course: adult-life
lesson: 59
title: The parents walk in: how good parents react
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When a parent finds out that a child has seen part of an adult film, the first second can be a shock for them too: a sharp voice, a grabbed remote, a red face. Then good parents calm down and do the important things: make sure the child is all right, make clear it is not their fault, and listen.
explain: Good parents ask how you feel, not only what you saw. They answer questions simply, or say "that's a good question, let me think how to explain it". They may tell you about a time it happened to them, and they thank you for telling.
explain: Vivid example: in Brisbane, Freya's mum comes into the lounge just as an 18 film appears on the screen, and Freya drops the remote. Mum switches the telly off, sits down and pulls her in. "You're not in trouble, possum. That came on by itself, didn't it? How are you feeling?" Freya bursts into tears, and then it's over much quicker than she feared.
explain: Afterwards, good families fix the cause, not the child: they set up a kids profile, turn off autoplay, use safe search, check the history together, have a word with other parents, and agree a family rule for next time. Shouting would only teach a child to hide things, so good parents try hard not to.
fact: A parent may sound sharp for a second from shock; that is about the shock, not about the child being bad.
fact: Good parents make clear that an accident is not the child's fault.
fact: A child can still feel bad after hearing "it's not your fault"; that feeling is normal and passes.
fact: Good parents ask how you feel, not only what you saw, and they listen.
fact: Parents may explain simply why a film is rated 18: it has things that are too strong or too grown-up for children.
fact: Some parents look embarrassed when a child asks about it; embarrassment is not anger, and asking was still right.
fact: When grown-ups talk quietly about what happened and say your name, they are usually working out how to help, not plotting a punishment.
fact: Parents may ring other parents after a sleepover; this is to keep all the children safe, not to get anyone in trouble.
fact: Parents may ask to see the chat or the TV history to understand what happened and fix it, not to spy.
fact: After an accident, families often set up a kids profile, turn off autoplay and turn on safe search.
fact: Many parents tell a story from their own childhood to show that it happens to everyone.
fact: Shouting teaches a child to hide things next time, so good parents try to stay calm.
fact: Thanking a child for telling makes it easier for them to tell again.
fact: A family rule like "if anything odd comes on, switch off and tell" makes the next time simple.
exercise: Freya's mum walks in just as an 18 film appears, and Freya drops the remote. What does a good parent do first? | Switch off, stay calm and check she's all right | Shout loudly so she remembers never to do it again | Send her to bed without asking anything | Keep watching to see what the film is
exercise: Jake's dad sees the 18 film on Jake's tablet and his voice goes sharp for a second. What does the sharpness usually mean? | Dad got a shock, not that Jake is bad | Jake is going to be punished for a year | Dad has been angry with Jake all day | Dad wanted to watch the film himself
exercise: Ruby's parents ask her to show them the chat with the link. Why? | To understand what happened and fix it | To read all her messages for fun | To find someone to shout at loudly | To send the link to other parents
exercise: Mum tells Alfie "It's not your fault," but he still feels bad. What's true? | Feeling bad after an accident is normal and passes | Mum must be lying to him if he still feels this bad | He feels bad because it really was his fault after all | Feeling bad means he should be punished
exercise: Grace asks why the film was rated 18. What might a good dad say? | It has things too strong or too grown-up for children | It is eighteen hours long, far too long for children to sit through | It was made eighteen years ago, so it's old | Eighteen people in it are famous film stars
exercise: Harry hears Grandma and Mum talking quietly about the old DVD, and his name comes up. What are they probably doing? | Working out how to help and what to change | Deciding on a very hard punishment for him | Laughing at him for being a little baby | Planning to throw away all the DVDs he likes
exercise: Poppy tells Mum about the sleepover film, and Mum rings the other parents. Why? | So all the children are safe and the parents know | So the older girl gets into as much trouble as possible | So Poppy is never invited anywhere ever again | So the other parents can buy the same film
exercise: Max's dad asks why he didn't wake him when the 18 film came on. What is Dad really saying? | Next time, wake me; I'd rather know | Max should have kept watching till the end | Dad is angry that Max let him sleep at all | Max is too old to need a grown-up's help
exercise: Sophie's mum asks if she has any questions about what she saw on the phone. Why? | So Sophie can understand and stop worrying | So Mum can test if Sophie was paying attention | So Sophie will tell everyone at school about it | So Mum can find out which phone is the best one
exercise: Oscar's dad doesn't shout but says, "We need to talk about the TV." What's likely? | A calm talk about what to change | A long punishment with no TV ever | A plan to sell the TV tomorrow | A talk about buying a bigger TV
exercise: Evie's parents set up a kids profile on the TV after her accident. What does that do? | It hides films that are too old for her | It records everything she watches for school | It makes all films finish after one hour | It turns the TV off whenever she's alone
exercise: Finn asks about the 18 film and his parents look embarrassed. What does that mean? | Grown-ups can feel awkward too; asking was right | Finn asked a bad question and should be ashamed | His parents must have made the film themselves | His parents are angry and will punish him later
exercise: Ava's mum says she can tell her anything, even embarrassing things. What does Mum want? | To hear about worries, not have them hidden | To know every single thing Ava does all day | To have funny stories to tell her friends | To check that Ava has nothing embarrassing
exercise: Leo's dad tells him about seeing a scary film too young. Why does he share it? | To show it happens to everyone and he understands | To boast that he watched films before Leo did | To teach Leo which scary films are the best | To change the subject so they don't talk about it
exercise: Mia's mum asks how she feels, not just what she saw. Why does that matter? | The feelings are what need looking after | The details of the film are none of her business | Feelings are easier to punish than actions | It's quicker than hearing the whole story
exercise: Noah's parents check the TV history after his accident. Why? | To see what happened and stop it happening again | To catch Noah out in a lie about cartoons | To pick a new film to watch together tonight | To delete every programme Noah has ever liked
exercise: Isla's mum hugs her and says, "Thank you for telling me." Why thank her? | So she knows telling was right and will tell again | Because Isla did Mum a big favour with chores | So Isla feels she must tell everyone else too | Because Mum is polite to everybody she meets
exercise: Charlie's mum says shouting would only make him hide things next time. What does she mean? | Children who get shouted at stop telling | Shouting is good for children if done softly | Children who hide things never get shouted at | Hiding things is fine as long as you are calm
test: Lily's dad asks her to help set up safe search. Why ask her to help? | So she understands it and is part of the fix | Because dads can't use computers at all | So she is punished with extra homework | Because safe search only works for children
test: Theo's parents agree a family rule: "If anything odd comes on, switch off and tell." Why is a rule like that useful? | Everyone knows exactly what to do next time | It makes sure nothing odd ever comes on again | It means the TV is switched off for good | It lets the parents shout without feeling bad
test: Ella's mum: "If it ever happens again, you won't be in trouble for telling me." What does Mum promise? | Telling won't get her into trouble | She'll never be in trouble for anything | She must tell only if it happens twice | It will never ever happen again
test: Mum on the phone to Auntie Sue: "No, I didn't go off at her, it wasn't her fault, the bloody autoplay did it." What happened? | Mum stayed calm and blamed the autoplay, not the child | Mum shouted at the child about the autoplay until she cried | Auntie Sue switched the autoplay on by mistake | Mum is cross with Auntie Sue for not helping
test: Dad, hissing to Mum in the kitchen: "We should've put a PIN on it months ago." Who is he blaming? | Himself and Mum, for not setting it up | Their child, for using the TV at all | The TV shop, for selling them the TV | The neighbours, for having a louder TV
test: Big brother: "Mum's gonna kill you when she finds out." Isla tells Mum and gets a hug. What was wrong with his idea? | Good parents comfort a child who tells, not punish | He was right, Mum was just pretending to be nice | Mums only hug when they're too tired to shout | He meant Mum would be very proud of Isla
test: At the school gate one mum says, "I rang Poppy's mum about the sleepover, she was ever so good about it." What does this mean? | The parents talked calmly to sort it out | The two mums had a big row at the gate | Poppy's mum didn't know about the sleepover | Poppy's mum is going to be in big trouble
test: Gran: "When I was your age I saw a horror film through the crack in the door. Gave me nightmares for a week!" Why does she tell this? | To show it happened to her and she got over it | To tell Jake he should watch films through doors | To show that films were scarier long ago | To blame Jake's mum for what happened to her
test: Your mate: "My mum was well embarrassed when I asked about it, so I won't ask again." What would you tell him? | Embarrassed isn't angry; asking was still right | He's right, never ask grown-ups anything awkward | His mum must be angry with him for weeks | He should ask other kids at school instead
test: Dad's voice goes sharp: "What's that? Off, now!" A minute later he says sorry and sits down with Jake. What does this show? | Even good parents get a shock, then calm down | Dad was angry with Jake and still is, deep down | Jake should feel ashamed for a long time | Sorry means Dad will let him watch it
test: A boy: "If your parents check your history, it means they don't trust you." Is that right here? | No, after an accident it's to understand and fix it | Yes, checking history always means they think you lie | Yes, parents never check anything for a good reason | No, history is checked only to find good films
exam: Identify the principal object of a parent who, upon learning that a child has viewed adult material by accident, enquires as to the child's feelings. | The care of the child's emotional wellbeing | The collection of evidence for a punishment | The satisfaction of the parent's curiosity | The assessment of the child's memory
exam: Determine the consequence which this lesson attributes to the habitual shouting at a child who discloses such an incident. | The child is likely to conceal future incidents | The child is likely to disclose more promptly | The child will thereby become better behaved | The child will thereafter avoid all screens
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning measures adopted by families subsequent to such an incident. | The installation of a children's profile and safe search | The permanent removal of all television from the home | The imposition of a prolonged punishment on the child | The prohibition of all visits to the homes of friends
exam: Whereas a parent has momentarily spoken sharply upon discovering the incident, ascertain the most probable explanation. | Surprise on the parent's part, not blame of the child | A settled conclusion that the child is wholly at fault | A long-standing grievance against the child concerned | A wish on the parent's part to view the film in question
